{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-109523",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We need to calculate $\\text{E}[X+Y].$\n\nRecall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables (not necessarily independent), and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nThe expected values of $X$ and $Y$ are calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot 0.1+1\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.2+3\\cdot 0.2=1.5 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & =0\\cdot 0.5+1\\cdot 0.3+2\\cdot 0.2=0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X+Y]=\\text{E}[X]+\\text{E}[Y]=1.5+0.7=2.2.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
